Found the root cause! 

In `UserDashboardScreen.tsx`:

```js
useEffect(() => {
    if (isFocused) {
      console.log('[Dashboard] Wallet hook data:', wallet);
      loadVerificationStatus();
      loadSubscriptionTimeline();
    }
  }, [isFocused, wallet, session?.user?.id]);
```

The effect depends on `wallet` but `wallet` is returned from `useSPWallet()` hook. Every time `wallet` changes (which triggers re-renders), this effect re-fires, which likely calls `loadVerificationStatus()` and `loadSubscriptionTimeline()` which update state, which triggers another render, changing wallet again, creating a loop.


The real problem is that `useSPWallet` probably returns a new object reference on each render, so even when the data hasn't actually changed, the effect sees a different wallet object and fires again—triggering API calls that update state and perpetuate the cycle.

